  "result": "Metabolic products of microorganisms. 224. Bafilomycins, a new group of macrolide antibiotics. Production, isolation, chemical structure and biological activity. (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6423597/)\nciteturn33search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; Production, isolation, chemical structure and biological activity. ... Publication date: 1984 Feb ... The bafilomycins A1, A2, B1, B2, C1 and C2, a new type of macrolide antibiotics with a 16-membered lactone ring, were isolated from the fermentation broth of three Streptomyces griseus strains (TU 1922, TU 2437, TU 2599) by ethyl acetate extraction and column chromatography on silica gel. The bafilomycins exhibit activity against Gram-positive bacteria and fungi.\n# Metabolic products of microorganisms. 224. Bafilomycins, a new group of macrolide antibiotics. Production, isolation, chemical structure and biological activity.\n\nThe Journal of antibiotics\n\nPMID: 6423597\n\nPublication date: 1984 Feb\n\nPublication status: ppublish\n\nJournal abbreviation: J Antibiot (Tokyo)\n\nJournal country: England\n\nJournal NLM unique ID: 0151115\n\nJournal linking ISSN: 0021-8820\n\nISSN: 0021-8820\n\nVolume: 37\n\nIssue: 2\n\nPages: 110-7\n\n## Identifiers\n\n  * DOI: 10.7164/antibiotics.37.110\n\n## Authors\n\n  * G Werner\n  * H Hagenmaier\n  * H Drautz\n  * A Baumgartner\n  * H Zähner\n\n## Languages\n\n  * eng\n\n## Publication types\n\n  * Journal Article\n  * Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe bafilomycins A1, A2, B1, B2, C1 and C2, a new type of macrolide antibiotics with a 16-membered lactone ring, were isolated from the fermentation broth of three Streptomyces griseus strains (TU 1922, TU 2437, TU 2599) by ethyl acetate extraction and column chromatography on silica gel. The bafilomycins exhibit activity against Gram-positive bacteria and fungi. Physico-chemical data, chemical structures and biological activities are reported.\n\n## MeSH terms\n\n  * Anti-Bacterial Agents / biosynthesis\n  * Anti-Bacterial Agents / isolation & purification\n  * Anti-Bacterial Agents / pharmacology\n  * Antifungal Agents / biosynthesis\n  * Antifungal Agents / isolation & purification\n  * Antifungal Agents / pharmacology\n  * Bacteria / drug effects\n  * Chemical Phenomena\n  * Chemistry\n  * Fermentation\n  * Fungi / drug effects\n  * Lactones / isolation & purification\n  * Macrolides\n  * Streptomyces griseus / metabolism\n\n## Substances\n\n  * Anti-Bacterial Agents\n  * Antifungal Agents\n  * Lactones\n  * Macrolides\n  * bafilomycin A1 (Registry number: 88899-55-2)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStudies on new phosphate ester antifungal antibiotics phoslactomycins. I. Taxonomy, fermentation, purification and biological activities (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2753808/)\nciteturn33search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; 1989 Jul;42(7):1019-25. ... S Fushimi^{  1 }, S Nishikawa, A Shimazu, H Seto ... New antibiotics phoslactomycins A, B, C, D, E and F, which contain alpha, beta-unsaturated delta-lactone, phosphate ester, conjugated diene and cyclohexane ring moieties, were isolated from the culture broth of a soil isolate actinomycete. ... Phoslactomycins were obtained by butanol extraction, gel filtration and reverse phase chromatography.\n\n### Full text links\n\nJ-STAGE, Japan Science and Technology Information Aggregator, Electronic\n\nFull text links\n\n. 1989 Jul;42(7):1019-25.\n\ndoi: 10.7164/antibiotics.42.1019.\n\n# Studies on new phosphate ester antifungal antibiotics phoslactomycins. I. Taxonomy, fermentation, purification and biological activities\n\nS Fushimi^{  1 }, S Nishikawa, A Shimazu, H Seto\n\nAffiliations Expand\n\n### Affiliation\n\n  * ^{1} Institute of Applied Microbiology, University of Tokyo, Japan.\n\n  * PMID: 2753808\n  * DOI: 10.7164/antibiotics.42.1019\nFree article\n\nItem in Clipboard\n\n# Studies on new phosphate ester antifungal antibiotics phoslactomycins. I. Taxonomy, fermentation, purification and biological activities\n\nS Fushimi et al. J Antibiot (Tokyo). 1989 Jul.\n\nFree article\n\nShow details\n\n\n. 1989 Jul;42(7):1019-25.\n\ndoi: 10.7164/antibiotics.42.1019.\n\n### Authors\n\nS Fushimi^{  1 }, S Nishikawa, A Shimazu, H Seto\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSTUDIES ON NEW PHOSPHATE ESTER ANTIFUNGAL ANTIBIOTICS PHOSLACTOMYCINS II. STRUCTURE ELUCIDATION OF PHOSLACTOMYCINS A TO F (https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/antibiotics1968/42/7/42_7_1026/_article)\nciteturn33search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 37.3 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; STUDIES ON NEW PHOSPHATE ESTER ANTIFUNGAL ANTIBIOTICS PHOSLACTOMYCINS ... SUSUMU FUSHIMI, KAZUO FURIHATA, HARUO SETO ... 1989 Volume 42 Issue 7 Pages 1026-1036\n\nSTUDIES ON NEW PHOSPHATE ESTER ANTIFUNGAL ANTIBIOTICS PHOSLACTOMYCINS\n\nII. STRUCTURE ELUCIDATION OF PHOSLACTOMYCINS A TO F\n\nSUSUMU FUSHIMI, KAZUO FURIHATA, HARUO SETO\n\nAuthor information\n\n  * SUSUMU FUSHIMI\n\nInstitute of Applied Microbiology, The University of Tokyo\n\n  * KAZUO FURIHATA\n\nInstitute of Applied Microbiology, The University of Tokyo\n\n  * HARUO SETO\n\nInstitute of Applied Microbiology, The University of Tokyo\n\n\nJOURNAL FREE ACCESS\n\n1989 Volume 42 Issue 7 Pages 1026-1036\n\nDOI https://doi.org/10.7164/antibiotics.42.1026\n\nDetails\n\n  * Published: July 25, 1989 Received: February 16, 1989 Available on J-STAGE: April 19, 2006 Accepted: - Advance online publication: - Revised: -\n\nDownload PDF (509K)\n\nArticle overview\n\n  * Abstract\n  * References (11)\n  * Content from these authors\n  * Cited by (61)\n\n\nAbstract\n\nPhoslactomycins A to F are new antifungal antibiotics produced by Streptomyces nigrescens SC-273. On the basis of physico-chemical properties and NMR studies, their structures have been determined as shown in Fig. 6. They are characterized by possessing α, β-unsaturated δ-lactone, phosphate ester, conjugated diene and cyclohexane ring moieties. The structural difference between them is ascribed to a substituent bound to the cyclohexane ring.\n\n\nReferences (11)\n\nRelated articles (0)\n\nFigures (0)\n\nImage Content from these authors\n\nCited by (61)\n\n© Japan Antibiotics Research Association--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTHE JOURNAL OF ANTIBIOTICS — JULY 1989 — 1026 (https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/antibiotics1968/42/7/42_7_1026/_pdf)\nciteturn33search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 20.7 years ago; Crawled: 19.0 years ago; THE JOURNAL OF ANTIBIOTICS — JULY 1989 — 1026 ... SUSUMU FUSHIMI, KAZUO FURIHATA and HARUO SETO* ... New antifungal antibiotics phoslactomycins A to F were obtained from the culture broth of *Streptomyces nigrescens* SC-273, a soil isolate. ... Physico-chemical properties of phoslactomycins A to F are summarized in Table 1.\nTHE JOURNAL OF ANTIBIOTICS — JULY 1989 — 1026\n\nSTUDIES ON NEW PHOSPHATE ESTER ANTIFUNGAL  \nANTIBIOTICS PHOSLACTOMYCINS\n\nII. STRUCTURE ELUCIDATION OF PHOSLACTOMYCINS A TO F\n\nSUSUMU FUSHIMI, KAZUO FURIHATA and HARUO SETO*  \nInstitute of Applied Microbiology, The University of Tokyo,  \nBunkyo-ku, Tokyo 113, Japan  \n(Received for publication February 16, 1989)\n\nPhoslactomycins A to F are new antifungal antibiotics produced by *Streptomyces nigrescens* SC-273. On the basis of physico-chemical properties and NMR studies, their structures have been determined as shown in Fig. 6. They are characterized by possessing α,β-unsaturated δ-lactone, phosphate ester, conjugated diene and cyclohexane ring moieties. The structural difference between them is ascribed to a substituent bound to the cyclohexane ring.\n\nNew antifungal antibiotics phoslactomycins A to F were obtained from the culture broth of *Streptomyces nigrescens* SC-273, a soil isolate. Taxonomy of the producing strain, fermentation, purification and biological activities of phoslactomycins have been reported in the preceding paper¹). --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTHE JOURNAL OF ANTIBIOTICS — FEB. 1984 (https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/antibiotics1968/37/2/37_2_110/_pdf)\nciteturn33search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 20.7 years ago; Crawled: 19.0 years ago; 1984 ... The bafilomycins exhibit activity against Gram-positive bacteria and fungi. ... In this paper we describe the fermentation (strain TÜ 1922), isolation, purification, chemical structure and biological properties of the bafilomycins, a new class of 16-membered macrolides.\nTHE JOURNAL OF ANTIBIOTICS — FEB. 1984\n\nMETABOLIC PRODUCTS OF MICROORGANISMS. 224†\n\nBAFILOMYCINS, A NEW GROUP OF MACROLIDE ANTIBIOTICS\n\nPRODUCTION, ISOLATION, CHEMICAL STRUCTURE  \nAND BIOLOGICAL ACTIVITY\n\nGerhard Werner and Hanspaul Hagenmaier*  \nInstitut für Organische Chemie der Universität Tübingen  \nAuf der Morgenstelle 18, D-74 Tübingen, G. F. R.\n\nHannelore Drautz, Angelika Baumgartner and Hans Zähner  \nInstitut für Biologie II, Lehrstuhl für Mikrobiologie I der Universität Tübingen,  \nAuf der Morgenstelle 18, D-74 Tübingen, G. F. R.\n\n(Received for publication August 31, 1983)\n\nThe bafilomycins A₁, A₂, B₁, B₂, C₁ and C₂, a new type of macrolide antibiotics with a 16-membered lactone ring, were isolated from the fermentation broth of three *Streptomyces griseus* strains (TÜ 1922, TÜ 2437, TÜ 2599) by ethyl acetate extraction and column chromatography on silica gel. The bafilomycins exhibit activity against Gram-positive bacteria and fungi. Physico-chemical data, chemical structures and biological activities are reported.\n\nThe strain, *Streptomyces griseus* sp. sulphurus (TÜ 1922) is a good example for the ability of microorganisms to produce various secondary metabolites, depending on the culture conditions. The recognition of these metabolites depends on the screening method used for their detection. Under certain culture conditions *Streptomyces griseus* produce mainly the iron-complex of tirandamycin B as biologically active compound¹). A change in culture conditions leads to the production of the Mg-complex of tirandamycin A²) and the bafilomycins as biological active compounds and pyrrol-2-carbonic acid, detected in a chemical screening procedure. In this paper we describe the fermentation (strain TÜ 1922), isolation, purification, chemical structure and biological properties of the bafilomycins, a new class of 16-membered macrolides.\n\n## Materials and Methods\n\n### Bacterial Strains\nThe standard strains for the activity spectrum of the bafilomycins were obtained from the stock culture collection in our laboratories or from ATCC. The antibiotic-producing microorganisms (TÜ 1922, TÜ 2437, TÜ 2599) were new soil isolates, classified according to Hütter and Bergey as *Streptomyces griseus*³,⁴).\n\n### Fermentation Studies\n* S. griseus* was cultured for 96 hours at 27°C in a medium (100 ml in a 500-ml Erlenmeyer flask with one intrusion) consisting of 2% meat meal, 2% malt extract, 1% CaCO₃ (NL 111). The pH was adjusted to 7.2 before autoclaving. These cultures were used as inoculum for the 10-liter fermentor. Bafilomycins were produced in a 10-liter fermentor under the following culture conditions: 5% inoculum was\n\n† Part 223: König, W.; H. Hahn, B. Fischer and H. Zähner: Identifizierung und Synthese von 2,5-Diamino-4-hydroxy-6-methylheptansäure, eines Abbauproduktes des Glutaminantagonisten aus *Nannizzia gypsea*. Liebigs Ann. Chem., in press.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDiss. ETH No. 17777 (https://www.research-collection.ethz.ch/bitstream/handle/20.500.11850/150520/eth-30617-02.pdf)\nciteturn33search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; bafilomycins A2, B2 and C2, were found during the isolation, and identified as the ... 1984, 37, 110.\nTotal Synthesis of Bafilomycin A1 \n \n1 \n \n \n \n \n \nIntroduction\n1\n \n \n \n1.1 Isolation of Bafilomycin A1  \n \nBafilomycin A1 (1) was first isolated in 1983 by Werner and Hagenmaier from the culture \nmedium of Streptomyces griseus sp. sulphurus, together with the structurally closely related \nnatural products bafilomycin B1 and C1 (Chart 1).1,2 Three additional compounds, named \nbafilomycins A2, B2 and C2, were found during the isolation, and identified as the \ncorresponding methyl ketals at C19.3 In contrast to the naturally occurring bafilomycin A1, B1 \nand C1, these products were artifacts, formed during the extraction procedure.  \n \nOMe Me\nMe\nOMe Me\nMe\n1\nO\nOH\nO\nOH\n23\niPr\nMe\nO\nOH\nO\nOH\nMe\nO\nOR3\nMe\nO\n19\n10\n15\nR2O\niPr\nR1O\nOMe\nMe\nOMe\nMe\nMe\nMe\nMe\nMe\nMe\nBafilomycin D: R3 = H\nBafilomycin A1: R1 = H, R2 = H\nO\nH\nN\nO\nH\nN\nO\nO\nBafilomycin E: R3 =\nBafilomycin B1: R1 = H, R2 =\nO\nO\nHO\nHO\nBafilomycin C1: R1 = H, R2 =\nOH\nO\nO\n \n \nChart 1. Structure of the bafilomycins.  \n \n                                                 \n1  Werner, G.; Hagenmaier, H.; Albert, K.; Kohlshorn, H.; Drautz, H. Tetrahedron Lett. 1983, 24, 5193.  \n2  Werner, G.; Hagenmaier, H.; Drautz, H.; Baumgartner, A.; Zähner, H. J. Antibiot. 1984, 37, 110.  \n3  The numbering scheme given in reference 1 will be used throughout the text.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCharacterization of the Bafilomycin Biosynthetic Gene Cluster from Streptomyces lohii (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3771327/)\nciteturn33search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.8 years ago; Crawled: last week; ^{1} This compound was first isolated from Streptomyces griseus sp. sulphurus (TÜ 1922) in 1984. ... ^{3,4} In addition, synergistic antifungal activity of bafilomycin A_{1} with the calcineurin inhibitor FK506 against the life-threatening fungal pathogen Cryptococcus neoformans,^{5} inhibition of the release of β-amyloid by bafilomycin A_{1},^{6} together with antitumor,^{7} antiparasitic,^{8} and immunosuppressant^{9} activities of other bafilomycins were reported.\n\n# Characterization of the Bafilomycin Biosynthetic Gene Cluster from Streptomyces lohii\n\nIssue date 2013 Feb 11.\n\nKeywords: bafilomycin, plecomacrolide, polyketide, biosynthesis, secondary metabolite\n\nPMC Copyright notice\n\nPMCID: PMC3771327  NIHMSID: NIHMS505201  PMID: 23362147\n\nBafilomycin B_{1} is the archetypal compound of the plecomacrolide family of natural products, which include concanamycins, viranamycins, hygrolidin, elaiophylin, and allied metabolites.^{1} This compound was first isolated from Streptomyces griseus sp. sulphurus (TÜ 1922) in 1984.^{2} The term plecomacrolide was later introduced to describe a subgroup of polyketides featured with a 16- or 18-membered macrolactone containing two conjugated diene units, and a hemiacetal side chain.^{1} These compounds have now been studied extensively as vacuolar ATPase (V-ATPase) inhibitors and, as such, have shown some promise as anti-osteoporetics.^{3,4} In addition, synergistic antifungal activity of bafilomycin A_{1} with the calcineurin inhibitor FK506 against the life-threatening fungal pathogen Cryptococcus neoformans,^{5} inhibition of the release of β-amyloid by bafilomycin A_{1},^{6} together with antitumor,^{7} antiparasitic,^{8} and immunosuppressant^{9} activities of other bafilomycins were reported. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLactomycins A–C, Dephosphorylated Phoslactomycin Derivatives That Inhibit Cathepsin B, from the Marine-Derived Streptomyces sp. ACT232 (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5852498/)\nciteturn33search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.7 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; [person-group-type: author] [name-style: western] Fushimi S. [name-style: western] Nishikawa S. [name-style: western] Shimazu A. [name-style: western] Seto H.\n\nStudies on new phosphate ester antifungal antibiotics phoslactomycins. ... Year: 1989 Volume: 42 First page: 1019 Last page: 1025 Identifier: [pub-id-type: doi] 10.7164/antibiotics.42.1019 Identifier: [pub-id-type: pmid] 2753808\n8. [publication-type: journal]\n\n[person-group-type: author] [name-style: western] Trost B.M. [name-style: western] Knopf J.D. [name-style: western] Brindle C.S.\n\nSynthetic strategies employed for the construction of fostriecin and related natural products Chem. Rev. Year: 2016 Volume: 116 First page: 15035 Last page: 15088 Identifier: [pub-id-type: doi] 10.1021/acs.chemrev.6b00488 Identifier: [pub-id-type: pmid] 28027648 Identifier: [pub-id-type: pmcid] PMC5720176\n\n9. [publication-type: journal]\n\n[person-group-type: author] [name-style: western] Wheeler C.R. [name-style: western] Trager W.F.\n\nAbsolute configuration of acenocoumarin J. Med. Chem. Year: 1979 Volume: 22 First page: 1122 Last page: 1124 Identifier: [pub-id-type: doi] 10.1021/jm00195a023 Identifier: [pub-id-type: pmid] 490560\n\n10. [publication-type: journal]\n\n[person-group-type: author] [name-style: western] Fushimi S. [name-style: western] Nishikawa S. [name-style: western] Shimazu A. [name-style: western] Seto H.\n\nStudies on new phosphate ester antifungal antibiotics phoslactomycins. I. Taxonomy, fermentation, purification and biological activities J Antibiot. Year: 1989 Volume: 42 First page: 1019 Last page: 1025 Identifier: [pub-id-type: doi] 10.7164/antibiotics.42.1019 Identifier: [pub-id-type: pmid] 2753808\n\n11. [publication-type: journal]\n\n[person-group-type: author] [name-style: western] Fushimi S. [name-style: western] Furihata K. [name-style: western] Seto H.\n\nStudies of new phosphate ester antifungal antibiotics phoslactomycins. II. Structure elucidation of phoslactomycins A to F J. Antibiot. (Tokyo) Year: 1989 Volume: 42 First page: 1026 Last page: 1036 Identifier: [pub-id-type: doi] 10.7164/antibiotics.42.1026 Identifier: [pub-id-type: pmid] 2753809\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStudies on new phosphate ester antifungal antibiotics phoslactomycins. II: Structure elucidation of phoslactomycins A to F (https://eurekamag.com/research/078/017/078017139.php)\nciteturn33search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; 1989: Studies on new phosphate ester antifungal antibiotics phoslactomycins. ... 2026: Synthetic Study of C1-C13 Intermediate in Phoslactomycins and Leustroducsins Chemical and Pharmaceutical Bulletin 74(1): 79-89】\n\n# Studies on new phosphate ester antifungal antibiotics phoslactomycins. II: Structure elucidation of phoslactomycins A to F\n\n##### Fushimi, S.; Furihata, K.; Seto, H.\n\n* * *\n\n#### Journal of Antibiotics (Tokyo 1968) 42(7): 1026-1036\n\n#### 1989\n\nISSN/ISBN: 0021-8820\n\nAccession: 078017139\n\n##### Full-Text Article emailed within 1 workday\n\n#### References\n\nFushimi, S.; Furihata, K.; Seto, H. 1989: Studies on new phosphate ester antifungal antibiotics phoslactomycins. II. Structure elucidation of phoslactomycins A to F Journal of Antibiotics 42(7): 1026-1036\n\n* * *\n\nFushimi, S.; Furihata, K.; Seto, H. 1989: Studies of new phosphate ester antifungal antibiotics phoslactomycins. Ii. Structure elucidation of phoslactomycins A to F The Journal of Antibiotics 42(7): 1026-1036\n\n* * *\n\nFushimi, S.; Nishikawa, S.; Shimazu, A.; Seto, H. 1989: Studies on new phosphate ester antifungal antibiotics phoslactomycins. I. Taxonomy, fermentation, purification and biological activities Journal of Antibiotics 42(7): 1019-1025\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIdentification of phoslactomycin biosynthetic gene clusters from Streptomyces platensis SAM-0654 and characterization of PnR1 and PnR2 as positive transcriptional regulators (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0378111912010311)\nciteturn33search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.0 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Phoslactomycins (PLMs) are a unique class of compounds produced by various soil Streptomyces, that contain an α,β-unsaturated δ-lactone, a tertiary alcohol at C8 substituted by a 2-aminoethyl chain, a phosphate at C9, a conjugated (Z,Z)-diene (C12-C15) with a cyclohexyl group at C15. PLMs A-F were isolated in 1989 from the culture broth of Streptomyces nigrescens with the potent activity against phytopathogenic fungi (Fushimi et al., 1989). ... PLMs are highly potent and selective inhibitors of protein serine/threonine phosphatase 2 A (PP2A), which is proposed to be responsible for their antitumor activity (Kawada et al., 2003, Teruya et al., 2005).\nBioinformatic analysis and inactivation experiment of regulatory genes suggest that PnR1 and PnR2 are two positive regulators of PLMs biosynthesis. Gene transcription analysis by reverse transcriptase PCR (RT-PCR) of the PLMs gene cluster demonstrated that PnR1 and PnR2 activate the transcription of the structural biosynthetic genes while PnR2 specially governs the transcription of pnR1 in a higher level.\n\n### Highlights\n\n► Two dispersed clusters for phoslactomycins were identified from S. platensis. ► Dissimilar construction of clusters from two different Streptomyces was detailed. ► Novel regulatory mechanism was confirmed by gene inactivation and RT-PCR analysis.\n\n## Introduction\n\nPhoslactomycins (PLMs) are a unique class of compounds produced by various soil Streptomyces, that contain an α,β-unsaturated δ-lactone, a tertiary alcohol at C8 substituted by a 2-aminoethyl chain, a phosphate at C9, a conjugated (Z,Z)-diene (C12-C15) with a cyclohexyl group at C15. PLMs A-F were isolated in 1989 from the culture broth of Streptomyces nigrescens with the potent activity against phytopathogenic fungi (Fushimi et al., 1989). --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBafilomycins: a class of inhibitors of membrane ATPases from microorganisms, animal cells, and plant cells. (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2973058/)\nciteturn33search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; # Bafilomycins: a c